### @Override

@Override is a compile-time instruction that tells the compiler to verify the annotated method actually overrides a method from a parent class or implements a method from an interface. If the method signature does not match any parent method, the compiler produces an error immediately, catching the bug before the code ever runs.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override  // compiler verifies this actually overrides a parent method
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
    }

    // @Override
    // public BigDecimal calulateInterest() { }  // COMPILE ERROR: typo, no such parent method
}
```

Always use @Override. It catches typos and accidental non-overrides at compile time. Without it, a typo silently creates a new method instead of overriding the parent, and polymorphism breaks.

### @Deprecated

@Deprecated marks a method, class, or field as outdated. The compiler generates a warning when any code calls the deprecated element. IDEs display it with a strikethrough. Since Java 9, the annotation has two optional elements: `since` records the version where deprecation started, and `forRemoval` signals whether the element will be deleted in a future release.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    @Deprecated(since = "2.0", forRemoval = true)
    public void transferLegacy(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Old implementation – use transfer() instead
    }

    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount) {
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
    }
}
```

When `forRemoval = true`, the compiler issues a stronger warning. In a banking codebase, marking legacy transfer methods as deprecated helps teams migrate gradually without breaking existing batch jobs that still call the old API.

### @SuppressWarnings

@SuppressWarnings tells the compiler to silence specific categories of warnings on the annotated element. Use it sparingly and only after confirming the warning is a false positive.

```java
@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
List<Account> accounts = (List<Account>) rawList;  // suppress unchecked cast warning

@SuppressWarnings({"deprecation", "unused"})
public void legacyBatchProcess() {
    transferLegacy("SAV2001", "CUR3001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
}
```

Common warning categories include `unchecked` (generic casts), `deprecation` (calling deprecated code), `unused` (unused variables), and `rawtypes` (raw generic types). Always add a comment explaining why the suppression is safe.

### @FunctionalInterface

@FunctionalInterface ensures the interface has exactly one abstract method, making it usable as a lambda target. If someone adds a second abstract method, the compiler reports an error.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

@FunctionalInterface  // compiler ensures exactly one abstract method
public interface TransferValidator {
    boolean isValid(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount);
    // Adding another abstract method here causes a COMPILE ERROR
}

// Used as a lambda in the transfer pipeline
TransferValidator positiveAmount = (from, to, amount) -> amount.signum() > 0;
TransferValidator sufficientFunds = (from, to, amount) ->
        from.getBalance().compareTo(amount) >= 0;
```

The interface can still have default methods and static methods. Only abstract methods count toward the single-method limit.

### @SafeVarargs

@SafeVarargs suppresses heap pollution warnings when a method accepts generic varargs. Heap pollution means a variable of a parameterized type refers to an object that is not of that type.

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountUtils {

    @SafeVarargs  // developer guarantees no heap pollution
    public static <T> List<T> listOf(T... elements) {
        return Arrays.asList(elements);
    }
}

// Usage
List<String> accountIds = AccountUtils.listOf("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "LOAN5001");
```

You may only apply @SafeVarargs to methods that are static, final, or private (since Java 9), because those cannot be overridden.

### Meta-annotations

Meta-annotations are annotations that you place on other annotations to control their behavior. Java provides five meta-annotations: @Target, @Retention, @Documented, @Inherited, and @Repeatable.

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Target(ElementType.METHOD)           // can only be placed on methods
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)   // available at runtime via reflection
@Documented                           // included in Javadoc
public @interface Audited {
    String action() default "";
}
```

| Retention | Available at | Used by |
|---|---|---|
| SOURCE | Compile time only | @Override, @SuppressWarnings |
| CLASS | In .class file, not runtime | Default if unspecified |
| RUNTIME | Via reflection at runtime | Spring, JPA, JUnit |

@Target restricts where the annotation can appear: METHOD, FIELD, TYPE, PARAMETER, CONSTRUCTOR, LOCAL_VARIABLE, ANNOTATION_TYPE, PACKAGE, TYPE_PARAMETER (Java 8+), and TYPE_USE (Java 8+).

### What happens if you use the wrong retention?

If you define a custom annotation with SOURCE retention but try to read it at runtime via reflection, the annotation is gone and reflection returns null. This is a common mistake when building custom framework features.

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)  // only available at compile time
public @interface CompileOnly { }

// At runtime:
Method m = TransferService.class.getMethod("transfer");
m.isAnnotationPresent(CompileOnly.class);  // false – the annotation was stripped
```

Always use RUNTIME retention when your code or a framework needs to discover the annotation via reflection.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **@Repeatable (Java 8+)** lets an annotation be used multiple times on the same element. It requires a container annotation to hold the repeated values.
- **Spring annotations (@Component, @Transactional)** use RUNTIME retention so Spring can read them via reflection during classpath scanning.
- **Annotation processing** (compile-time): Lombok, MapStruct, and Dagger generate code by processing annotations at compile time using JSR 269 processors.
- **TYPE_USE annotations (Java 8+)** can annotate any use of a type: `List<@NonNull String>`, `(@NonNull String) obj`. This is the foundation for tools like the Checker Framework that catch nullability bugs at compile time.

sections.s3_flow:
```text
  Built-in annotations:
    @Override           → compile check: actually overrides parent
    @Deprecated         → marks outdated code, compiler warns
    @SuppressWarnings   → silences specific warnings
    @FunctionalInterface→ exactly one abstract method
    @SafeVarargs        → suppress generic varargs warning

  Meta-annotations (on annotations):
    @Target    → where: METHOD, FIELD, TYPE, PARAMETER
    @Retention → when: SOURCE, CLASS, RUNTIME
    @Documented, @Inherited, @Repeatable
```
